faqs:
  - q: "How does the Word & Character Counter calculate word count?"
    a: "The tool uses Unicode-aware word tokenization (matching consecutive letters and digits across all alphabets, including accented characters). Hyphens and punctuation boundaries are parsed in accordance with standard typographical conventions."
  - q: "What is the difference between characters with and without spaces?"
    a: "Characters with spaces count every glyph, whitespace gap, and newline. Characters without spaces exclude whitespace gaps, counting only visible printable characters."
  - q: "How are reading and speaking times estimated?"
    a: "Reading time is based on the standard adult reading benchmark of 225 words per minute (wpm), established in cognitive psychology studies. Speaking time uses a conversational presentation benchmark of 140 words per minute."
  - q: "Why are common stop words excluded from keyword density?"
    a: "Common grammatical words like 'the', 'is', 'at', and 'and' occur frequently in nearly all English text. Filtering out stop words reveals the meaningful topical keywords in your content."
  - q: "Is my typed text saved or transmitted to a server?"
    a: "No. All text analysis runs 100% locally in your browser memory. Your writing is never transmitted, stored, or analyzed on any external server."
sources:
